CAT = "rsttmEngine"; SEG = "ENG"
Q = [
# base engine
("Blue smoke and high oil use appear on a diesel with good compression. What is a likely cause?",
 "Worn valve guides", ["Leaking injector tips", "A stuck-closed thermostat", "A restricted air filter"],
 "Oil drawn past worn valve stems burns in the cylinder and makes blue smoke while compression stays normal. Leaking injectors give black or white smoke from fuel, not oil."),
("Cylinder liner projection above the block deck is measured. Why does it matter?",
 "It sets head gasket clamping", ["It sets valve lash", "It sets piston-to-valve clearance", "It sets injector tip height"],
 "Wet liners must stand slightly proud of the deck so the head gasket clamps each liner evenly. Too little projection lets combustion gas and coolant leak."),
("A crankcase blow-by test shows high flow. What does that point to?",
 "Ring or liner wear", ["A plugged breather tube", "A weak water pump", "A sticking EGR valve"],
 "Blow-by is combustion gas slipping past the rings into the crankcase. High flow means ring, liner or piston wear, or in some engines a leaking air compressor."),
("Valve lash is set on a diesel cylinder. Where must that cylinder's piston be?",
 "Near TDC on compression", ["Near TDC on exhaust overlap", "At bottom dead centre", "Anywhere with the engine cold"],
 "Lash is set with the follower on the cam's base circle, which happens near TDC compression when both valves are closed. On overlap both valves are partly open."),
# lubrication
("Engine oil analysis shows high sodium and potassium. What does that suggest?",
 "Coolant entering the oil", ["Fuel diluting the oil", "Dirt entering the intake", "Normal bearing wear"],
 "Sodium and potassium come from coolant additives, so their rise points to a leaking oil cooler, liner seal or head gasket. Silicon points to dirt and fuel shows as dilution."),
("A full-flow oil filter is plugged. What does its bypass valve do?",
 "Passes unfiltered oil", ["Shuts the engine down", "Dumps oil back to the pan", "Raises the relief valve setting"],
 "The bypass opens on high pressure drop so the bearings still get oil, even unfiltered. That is why filter change intervals matter."),
("Why is engine oil sampled from the same point at the same interval each time?",
 "So results trend reliably", ["So the filter lasts longer", "So the lab can skip tests", "So the oil level stays full"],
 "Oil analysis is judged by how wear metals and contaminants change over time. Sampling the same way each time keeps readings comparable, so a rising trend stands out."),
# intake
("A charge air cooler leaks on a turbocharged diesel. Which symptom follows?",
 "Low boost and black smoke", ["High boost and white smoke", "Coolant loss through the exhaust", "Oil in the intake manifold"],
 "Boost escaping from the cooler or its hoses leaves too little air for the fuel, so power drops and the exhaust smokes. A pressure test of the CAC finds the leak."),
("A turbocharger shaft has some radial play but no wheel rub. What is done?",
 "Compare it with the OEM limit", ["Replace the turbo right away", "Tighten the centre housing bolts", "Shim the compressor housing"],
 "Journal-bearing turbos have some normal radial play. Measure it against the manufacturer's limit; wheel rub or excessive end play means the unit is worn out."),
("The vanes of a variable geometry turbocharger stick in one position. What does the ECM usually do?",
 "Log a boost fault and derate", ["Raise idle speed to clear it", "Shut off EGR flow for good", "Switch the fan to full speed"],
 "The ECM commands VGT vane position to hit a boost target, so a stuck actuator or vanes sets a boost or actuator code and limits power."),
# exhaust / aftertreatment
("A DPF loads up with soot much faster than normal. What should be checked first?",
 "The source of the excess soot", ["The DEF tank level", "The DPF outlet clamp torque", "The SCR inlet NOx sensor"],
 "Fast soot loading is a symptom: leaking injectors, low boost or EGR faults upstream make the soot. Fix the cause before cleaning or replacing the filter."),
("What does the SCR catalyst use DEF for?",
 "Reducing NOx emissions", ["Burning soot in the DPF", "Cooling the exhaust gas", "Oxidizing unburned fuel"],
 "DEF breaks down to ammonia, which reacts with nitrogen oxides across the SCR catalyst to form nitrogen and water. Soot is burned in the DPF."),
("Why is a diesel oxidation catalyst placed ahead of the DPF?",
 "Its heat helps regeneration", ["It stores ammonia for the SCR", "It traps ash from the oil", "It lowers DPF inlet temperature"],
 "The DOC oxidizes fuel and hydrocarbons in the exhaust, and the heat released raises the DPF inlet temperature enough to burn soot."),
("A DEF sample tests at the wrong concentration. What is done with it?",
 "Drain it and refill fresh", ["Dilute it until it reads right", "Add a DEF conditioner", "Run it out before refilling"],
 "Off-spec DEF causes NOx faults and derates and can damage the system. Drain it, flush if the OEM says so, and refill with fluid that meets the standard."),
# engine management
("Why does a diesel ECM use a camshaft position signal as well as a crank signal?",
 "To find which cylinder is firing", ["To measure engine load", "To set idle speed", "To time the fan clutch"],
 "The crank sensor gives position and speed, but each crank angle repeats every 360 degrees. The cam signal tells the ECM which cylinder is on its compression stroke so it fires the right injector."),
("A five-volt reference circuit is shorted to ground. What happens to the sensors sharing it?",
 "Several set codes at once", ["Only one sensor sets a code", "They read slightly high", "Nothing until the key cycles"],
 "Sensors on one reference circuit share the ECM supply, so a short pulls it down and several sensors set codes together. Unplug them one at a time to find the short."),
("What does a throttle position sensor send to the ECM?",
 "A varying voltage", ["A fixed 12 V supply", "A coolant temperature value", "An ignition pulse"],
 "The accelerator pedal sensor is a potentiometer: its signal voltage rises with pedal travel. Dual-track sensors send a second signal for the ECM to compare."),
("A replacement ECM is installed. What is usually needed before the engine runs right?",
 "Calibration download", ["Bleeding the fuel filters", "Resetting valve lash", "Relearning the tachograph"],
 "Replacement modules come blank or generic and must be loaded with the engine's calibration and vehicle settings using the OEM's software."),
# fuel
("Water is found in the fuel water separator bowl. What is done?",
 "Drain it and find the source", ["Add alcohol to the tank", "Replace the injection pump", "Change only the primary filter"],
 "Water damages injectors and pumps. Drain the separator, then check tanks, caps and the fuel supply to see how the water got in."),
("After a fuel filter change, the diesel will not start. What should be done?",
 "Prime the fuel system", ["Replace the starter relay", "Check the injector cut-outs", "Recharge the batteries"],
 "Air in the filters stops fuel reaching the injectors. Use the hand primer or electric lift pump to purge air as the OEM directs."),
("Why is a high-pressure common rail line never loosened with the engine running?",
 "Fuel can inject into skin", ["Air locks the transfer pump", "The rail pressure sensor fails", "The injectors lose their trim codes"],
 "Common rail pressure is high enough to pierce skin and cause injection injuries. Shut down and wait for pressure to bleed off before opening lines."),
("A misfire is traced to one cylinder. Which test isolates a weak injector?",
 "A cylinder cutout test", ["A cooling system pressure test", "A boost leak test", "A fuel tank vent check"],
 "Cutting out each injector in turn with the service tool shows which cylinder adds no power. A weak cylinder changes engine speed little when it is cut out."),
("Diesel fuel waxes in cold weather. What symptom fits?",
 "Plugged filters and starvation", ["White smoke from coolant", "High fuel rail pressure", "Hard starting when hot"],
 "Below its cloud point, diesel forms wax crystals that plug filters and starve the engine. Winter-blend fuel and fuel heaters prevent it."),
# retarder
("A compression brake fires but gives weak retarding. Which check comes first?",
 "Slave piston lash", ["Coolant overflow level", "Exhaust back pressure valve", "Battery state of charge"],
 "Engine brakes depend on a precise slave piston to valve clearance. Too much lash opens the valve late and retarding power drops."),
("Why does an engine brake stop retarding when the clutch is disengaged?",
 "The engine is uncoupled", ["The turbo overspeeds", "The solenoids lose power", "The fuel shut-off opens"],
 "Engine and exhaust brakes slow the truck by loading the engine. With the clutch disengaged the drivetrain no longer turns the engine, so there is no retarding."),
("An engine brake works but will not switch off. What is a likely cause?",
 "A stuck solenoid valve", ["Low engine oil viscosity", "A worn slave piston bore", "A weak exhaust valve spring"],
 "The brake solenoid sends oil to the slave pistons when energized. A stuck solenoid or shorted control switch keeps it applied; check the control circuit first."),
# cooling
("A cooling system pressure cap opens too early. What does that cause?",
 "Coolant boils sooner", ["Oil pressure rises", "The thermostat sticks", "The water pump cavitates less"],
 "System pressure raises the coolant's boiling point. A cap that opens too early lets coolant boil and push out of the overflow."),
("Why do some diesels with wet liners need supplemental coolant additive?",
 "To prevent cavitation pitting", ["To raise the freezing point", "To lubricate the thermostat", "To dye coolant for leak checks"],
 "Vibrating wet liners create bubbles that implode and pit the liner. SCA forms a protective film; extended-life coolants carry their own inhibitors."),
("A viscous fan clutch stays locked up even when the engine is cold. What results?",
 "Constant fan roar", ["Overheating at highway speed", "A slipping fan belt", "Low heater output on idle"],
 "A fan clutch locked on turns the fan at full speed all the time. The engine runs cool but noisy and burns more fuel."),
("Combustion gas bubbles appear in the coolant overflow. Which test confirms the source?",
 "A chemical block test", ["An oil pressure test", "A coolant freeze point test", "A fuel return flow test"],
 "A block tester changes colour when exhaust gases are drawn from the coolant, pointing to a head gasket, cracked head or liner seal."),
("A thermostat is stuck open on a highway tractor. What is the effect?",
 "The engine runs too cool", ["Coolant boils over", "Radiator hoses collapse", "The fan clutch stays off"],
 "With the thermostat open, coolant flows through the radiator all the time, so the engine runs cold, heater output is poor and fuel economy drops."),
("Two incompatible coolant types are mixed by mistake. What is the main risk?",
 "Inhibitors can drop out and gel", ["The freezing point rises above zero", "The pressure cap stops sealing", "The SCA reading doubles"],
 "Some coolant chemistries are not compatible; mixed, their inhibitors can drop out and plug passages. Flush and refill with the OEM-specified coolant."),
]
